<title>GERMANY: Dresdner sees 10 states at EMU start.</title>
<headline>Dresdner sees 10 states at EMU start.</headline>
<dateline>BERLIN 1997-07-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Germany's Dresdner Bank AG on Tuesday predicted that at least ten European countries would take part in the planned launch of the region's single currency in January 1999.</p>
<p>Chief executive Juergen Sarrazin told a news conference that the participating countries were likely to meet all the Maastricht Treaty conditions, with deficit to gross domestic product (GDP) ratios -- the key fiscal requirement -- of &quot;about&quot; the treaty reference level of three percent.</p>
<p>Sarrazin said he did not expect a spectacular market reaction to the decision on which countries can particpate in Europe's economic and monetary union (EMU) since most market players seemed to expect Dresdner's scenario.</p>
<p>&quot;To that extent one should not view the current favouring on the dollar as a one-sided expectation of euro weakness,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
